protests heat up for a second night  across barcelona  police stations banks and several stores  are damaged during demonstrations  thousands took to the streets to demand  the release of pablo hassell from jail  the rapper's lyrics and tweets got him  in hot water with the authorities  after he insulted spanish royalty  supported a now defunct  extremist group and criticized the  police  i'm a musician and it affects me the  fact that in spain  artists musicians rappers everyone is  arrested  i find it quite brutal that people are  arrested for the lyrics of a song  common sense tells me that i have to be  here because we have less and less  freedom in spain  on monday police stormed yada university  where hassell had barricaded himself in  with other activists  to avoid a jail sentence hassel was  taken away by police but remained  defiant  they will never make us give in despite  the repression  hassel's case has inspired support  across the country  200 prominent artists actors and  musicians  signed a petition for his release they  say  the so-called gag order prevents free  speech  spain's left-wing government has moved  to change the law  but that's come too late for herself  here say they'll continue to raise their  voices against the silencing  of the outspoken rapper  laura about manly al jazeera
 

